---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
---

<Layout
    title="See You Tomorrow — Aaron at 33 | Making Of"
    description="How a one-shot AI film pipeline turned a WhatsApp archive, a photo album, and $2 of API calls into a birthday film for my twin brother."
>
    <main class="container mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 py-12 sm:py-16">
        <article class="prose prose-lg dark:prose-invert mx-auto max-w-3xl">
            <header class="mb-10 text-center not-prose">
                <h1 class="text-4xl font-extrabold tracking-tight text-text-light dark:text-text-dark sm:text-5xl">
                    See You Tomorrow
                </h1>
                <p class="mt-3 text-xl italic text-gray-500 dark:text-gray-400">
                    Aaron at 33 — a film by his favorite twin brother
                </p>
            </header>

            <div class="not-prose mb-12">
                <video
                    controls
                    preload="metadata"
                    poster="/aaron33/poster.jpg"
                    class="w-full rounded-xl shadow-2xl"
                >
                    <source src="/aaron33/see-you-tomorrow.mp4" type="video/mp4" />
                    Your browser does not support the video tag.
                </video>
                <p class="mt-2 text-center text-sm text-gray-500">3:46 · 1080p · July 7, 2026</p>
            </div>

            <h2>The brief</h2>
            <p>
                My twin brother Aaron turned 33 on July 7, 2026 — our birthday. In August my family
                makes aliyah to Raanana, Israel, so this was the last birthday we'd spend as neighbors.
                I gave Claude (Anthropic's Fable 5, running in Claude Code) a folder and one instruction:
                make a 1–5 minute film celebrating Aaron, mix real footage with AI, use Whisper on the
                WhatsApp voice notes, budget $10, <em>make the viewer cry</em>.
            </p>
            <p>
                Everything below — research, script, shot selection, AI generation, edit, and mix —
                was done by the agent in a single session, in about two hours.
            </p>

            <h2>The raw material</h2>
            <ul>
                <li>A 6&nbsp;GB photo/video archive (457 files) — including photos of physical
                    photo-album pages from the 1990s</li>
                <li>A full WhatsApp chat export: <strong>9,593 messages</strong> between the two of us
                    from January to July 2026, plus 1,900+ photos, videos, and voice notes</li>
                <li>Whisper (faster-whisper, running locally) transcribed every voice note — which is
                    how the film found its cold open: a real voice note that begins
                    <em>“Hello good sir, it's your favorite twin brother.”</em></li>
            </ul>
            <p>
                The agent built contact sheets of all 309 photos and 130 videos, read them visually,
                and cast the film from them — the scanned photo of us as toddlers with
                <em>two birthday cakes</em> became the emotional anchor. Data-mining the chat produced the
                stats card: 120 “good sir”s, 98 “love”s, 91 conversations about Claude. And the climax
                was sitting in the archive all along — a real exchange from Purim:
                <em>“Plz come our last Purim together”</em> →
                <em>“see you tomorrow and next year in raanana inshallah”</em> → <em>“Amen!”</em>
            </p>

            <h2>The AI shots (Gemini Omni Flash)</h2>
            <p>
                Six video generations via <code>gemini-omni-flash-preview</code> (the Interactions API).
                Five text-to-video establishing shots and one image-to-video resurrection: the 1995
                two-cakes photograph, animated so the toddlers actually blow out their candles.
            </p>
            <p>Sample prompt (the opening shot):</p>
            <blockquote>
                <p>
                    Vintage 16mm film, summer morning, July 1993. Toronto skyline with the CN Tower,
                    soft golden haze, slow cinematic push-in toward a hospital building window. Film
                    grain, warm nostalgic color, gentle ambient city sounds, soft hopeful piano. Single
                    continuous shot, no scene cuts, no dialogue, no text on screen.
                </p>
            </blockquote>
            <p>And the image-to-video prompt for the two-cakes photo:</p>
            <blockquote>
                <p>
                    Bring this old 1990s family photograph gently to life: the candle flames flicker,
                    the two toddlers lean in and blow out the candles on their two birthday cakes, the
                    adults smile and clap. Subtle, warm, realistic home-video motion. Keep the vintage
                    90s film look, colors and everyone's faces exactly the same. Single continuous
                    shot, no scene cuts.
                </p>
            </blockquote>
            <p>
                The nursery shot — two bassinets labeled <strong>MILLER A</strong> and
                <strong>MILLER B</strong>, the babies reaching toward each other — came back perfect
                on the first try.
            </p>

            <h2>Voice, music, and the mix</h2>
            <ul>
                <li><strong>Narration:</strong> ~340 words written from my point of view, voiced with
                    <code>minimax/speech-02-hd</code> on Replicate (7 segments, ~2.9s average generation time)</li>
                <li><strong>Score:</strong> two 30-second cues from <code>meta/musicgen</code>
                    (stereo-large) — nostalgic piano for the childhood acts, hopeful swell for the
                    ending — looped with crossfades into a full bed</li>
                <li><strong>Real audio:</strong> Aaron's actual voice notes, a playground vlog
                    (“one, two, three, go!”), and my daughters shouting “Hi, Uncle!” over the credits</li>
                <li><strong>Mix:</strong> a single ffmpeg filtergraph — music side-chain compressed
                    (ducked) under every voice, limited, and muxed against the picture</li>
            </ul>

            <h2>The edit pipeline</h2>
            <p>
                No editing software — the whole cut is Python + ffmpeg, fully scripted and resumable:
            </p>
            <ol>
                <li>Every photo becomes a 1920×1080 “plate” (blur-fill background) with a Ken Burns
                    zoom via <code>zoompan</code></li>
                <li>Typographic cards (title, WhatsApp bubbles, stats, end cards) rendered with PIL —
                    Georgia for the emotional cards, Avenir Next for the data</li>
                <li>All 44 shots render to uniform silent MP4s, concat-muxed into picture lock</li>
                <li>The audio mix is computed from the same timeline data, so every voiceover and
                    voice note lands on its intended frame</li>
            </ol>

            <h2>What it cost</h2>
            <div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto">
                <table class="w-full text-sm border-collapse">
                    <thead>
                        <tr class="border-b border-gray-300 dark:border-gray-700 text-left">
                            <th class="py-2 pr-4">Item</th>
                            <th class="py-2 pr-4">Usage</th>
                            <th class="py-2">Approx. cost</th>
                        </tr>
                    </thead>
                    <tbody class="divide-y divide-gray-200 dark:divide-gray-800">
                        <tr><td class="py-2 pr-4">Gemini Omni Flash (video gen)</td><td class="py-2 pr-4">6 × 10s clips, 720p</td><td class="py-2">~$1–2 (preview pricing)</td></tr>
                        <tr><td class="py-2 pr-4">MiniMax speech-02-hd (Replicate)</td><td class="py-2 pr-4">7 segments, ~2,200 chars</td><td class="py-2">~$0.15</td></tr>
                        <tr><td class="py-2 pr-4">MusicGen stereo-large (Replicate)</td><td class="py-2 pr-4">2 runs, ~88s GPU each</td><td class="py-2">~$0.25</td></tr>
                        <tr><td class="py-2 pr-4">Whisper transcription</td><td class="py-2 pr-4">15 voice notes/audio files</td><td class="py-2">$0 (local)</td></tr>
                        <tr class="font-semibold"><td class="py-2 pr-4">Total</td><td class="py-2 pr-4">vs. a $10 budget</td><td class="py-2">≈ $2</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
            </div>

            <h2>Why it works</h2>
            <p>
                The AI shots are connective tissue, not the substance. Every emotional beat in the film
                is <em>real</em>: the scanned photographs, the voice notes, the chat messages, the
                statistics of an actual friendship. The models just gave the memories a camera.
            </p>
            <p class="italic">
                Happy birthday, Aaron. Next year in Raanana. Amen.
            </p>
        </article>
    </main>
</Layout>
